  "query_preview": "/Through Friday/...\nIssued at 1113 PM MDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nWe're off to a chilly start to the evening across the Platte River\nvalley and some localized portions of the I-25 corridor. A few of\nour typical cold spots in the plains have already dipped below\nfreezing, and a hard freeze is still expected across most of the\nnortheastern plains overnight tonight. Temperatures should remain\nin the mid 30s to low 40s across most of the I-25 corridor, but\nthere could be some patchy frost overnight.\n\nTemperatures are expected to warm into the mid 70s across the\nplains on Saturday as the upper level ridge remains over the\nregion. The airmass is exceptionally dry and it will be difficult\nto even muster a cloud during the day. \n\nThe ridge is expected to push off to the east by Sunday, as a weak\ncutoff low transitions back to an open wave, eventually drifting\ntowards the forecast area under weak southwesterly flow. The only\nnotable change in the weather will be an increase in cloud cover\nduring the day Sunday, but temperatures are still expected to\nremain in the 70s. \n\nThat shortwave should move through our forecast area by Monday,\nbringing a slight chance of rain/snow showers to the higher\nterrain, while the plains remain mild and dry. A few showers could\ncontinue through mid-week with moisture lingering in the south-\nsouthwesterly flow aloft.\n\nBy the latter half of the week, the chances for a more organized\nstorm system increase. A strong cutoff low is expected to be\ncentered over southern California by Thursday, which then shifts\neast towards Colorado by late week or sometime next weekend. As is\nstands right now, the majority of deterministic and ensemble\nguidance keeps almost all of the meaningful precipitation to the\nsouth and west of our forecast area. Of course, this potential\nstorm system is still about a week out, and it's likely we'll see\nshifts in the storm track over the next several days.",
